{{!
    @template local_moodesk/composer_attachments

    The attachment control for a form that composes a message: one button that
    opens Moodle's file picker, and the files the draft currently holds.

    Used by the New ticket form on both of its mounts — the drawer over the list
    and the standalone submit.php page — so the two cannot drift apart. The reply
    composer draws the same idea differently (a disclosure in its action bar,
    because a reply sits under a conversation and every line it claims at rest is
    a line of thread the agent cannot see); what the two share is everything
    below the surface: this template's file list, amd/src/composer_attachments.js,
    the draft endpoints in ajax.php and draft_attachment_service.

    The button is the upload, not a disclosure: clicking it opens the picker.
    The list under it is drawn from what the server reports the draft holds,
    never from what was just clicked, so a refused upload or a failed delete
    leaves the list telling the truth.

    It renders inside the <form> — moodleform places it with an 'html' element,
    which emits the markup verbatim with no .fitem wrapper of its own, so it can
    sit directly under the message box and share its left edge. Nothing here
    submits: what the submit needs is the draft item id, which the form carries
    in a hidden field.

    Context variables required for this template:
    * rootid   - Unique DOM id for the region. Two composers can share a screen
                 (the ticket list hosts this drawer and the ticket detail one),
                 so each is addressable on its own.
    * panelid  - Unique DOM id for the file list region.
    * label    - Visible button text, e.g. "Attach files". Never icon-only: the
                 paperclip is decoration, the word is the meaning.
    * maxfiles - How many files the form accepts, for the limit note.
    * config   - JSON the module reads to wire this control: the draft item id,
                 the endpoint, the sesskey and the file picker options. It rides
                 on the element rather than on a js_call_amd argument because
                 the picker options alone run past the 1024 characters Moodle
                 accepts there.

    Example context (json):
    {
        "rootid": "mhd-attach-65f1c2a0b3d4e",
        "panelid": "mhd-attach-panel-65f1c2a0b3d4e",
        "label": "Attach files",
        "maxfiles": 5,
        "config": "{\"draftitemid\":123}"
    }
}}
<div class="mhd-attach" id="{{rootid}}" data-region="mhd-composer-attachments"
     data-config="{{config}}">
  {{! The Button component, outlined at xsmall: attaching is a control that
      belongs to the field above it, never the form's primary action. }}
  <button type="button"
          class="mhd-btn mhd-btn--outlined mhd-btn--xsmall mhd-attach__trigger"
          data-action="mhd-attach-file"
          aria-controls="{{panelid}}">
    <svg class="mhd-btn__icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <use href="#mhd-icon-paperclip"></use>
    </svg>
    <span class="mhd-attach__label">{{label}}</span>
    <span class="mhd-attach__count" data-region="mhd-attach-count" hidden></span>
  </button>
  {{! Renders empty and stays empty until the draft holds something: a ticket
      with nothing attached costs the form no height. }}
  <div class="mhd-attach__panel" id="{{panelid}}" data-region="mhd-attach-panel">
    <div data-region="mhd-attach-list"></div>
  </div>
  <p class="mhd-attach__note">{{#str}} attachment_panel_note_inline, local_moodesk, {{maxfiles}} {{/str}}</p>
</div>
